n":[{"type":"text","value":"What is Rate Limiting?\nRate limiting allows a function to execute up to a limit within a time window,\nthen blocks all subsequent calls until the window passes. This can lead to \"bursty\" behavior where\nall executions happen immediately, followed by a complete block."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Window Types:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"'fixed': A strict window that resets after the window period. All executions within the window count\ntowards the limit, and the window resets completely after the period."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"'sliding': A rolling window that allows executions as old ones expire. This provides a more\nconsistent rate of execution over time."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuration Options:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"limit"},{"type":"text","value":": Maximum number of executions allowed within the window (required)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"window"},{"type":"text","value":": Time window in milliseconds (required)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"windowType"},{"type":"text","value":": 'fixed' or 'sliding' (default: 'fixed')"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether the rate limiter is enabled (default: true)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"asyncRetryerOptions"},{"type":"text","value":": Configure retry behavior for executions"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to Use Rate Limiting:\nRate limiting is best used for hard API limits or resource constraints. For UI updates or\nsmoothing out frequent events, throttling or debouncing usually provide better user experience."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A rate limiter will allow all executions until the limit is reached, then block all subsequent calls until the window resets"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A throttler ensures even spacing between executions, which can be better for consistent performance"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A debouncer collapses multiple calls into one, which is better for handling bursts of events"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Error Handling:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":" handler is provided, it will be called with the error and rate limiter instance"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":" is true (default when no onError handler is provided), the error will be thrown"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":" is false (default when onError handler is provided), the error will be swallowed"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both onError and throwOnError can be used together - the handler will be called before any error is thrown"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The error state can be checked using the underlying AsyncRateLimiter instance"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rate limit rejections (when limit is exceeded) are handled separately from execution errors via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onReject"},{"type":"text","value":" handler"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State Management:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Uses TanStack Store for reactive state management"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" to provide initial state values when creating the rate limiter"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" can be a partial state object"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSuccess"},{"type":"text","value":" callback to react to successful function execution and implement custom logic"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":" callback to react to function execution errors and implement custom error handling"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSettled"},{"type":"text","value":" callback to react to function execution completion (success or error) and implement custom logic"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onReject"},{"type":"text","value":" callback to react to executions being rejected when rate limit is exceeded"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The state includes execution times, success/error counts, and current execution status"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State can be accessed via the underlying AsyncRateLimiter instance's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"store.state"},{"type":"text","value":" property"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When using framework adapters (React/Solid), state is accessed from the hook's state property"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-parameters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Parameters"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"tfn","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TFn"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFn"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"extends"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"link","href":"../type-aliases/AnyAsyncFunction.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AnyAsyncFunction"}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"parameters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Parameters"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"fn","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fn"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFn"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"initialoptions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"initialOptions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../interfaces/AsyncRateLimiterOptions.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AsyncRateLimiterOptions"}]},{"type":"text","value":"<"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFn"},{"type":"text","value":">"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"returns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Returns"}]},{"type":"code","value":"(...args): Promise<Awaited<ReturnType<TFn>> | undefined>;","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Attempts to execute the rate-limited function if within the configured limits.\nWill reject execution if the number of calls in the current window exceeds the limit."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Error Handling:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the rate-limited function throws and no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":" handler is configured,\nthe error will be thrown from this method."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":" handler is configured, errors will be caught and passed to the handler,\nand this method will return undefined."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The error state can be checked using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getErrorCount()"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getIsExecuting()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"parameters-2","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Parameters"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"args","children":[{"type":"text","value":"args"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"..."},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Parameters"},{"type":"text","value":"<"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFn"},{"type":"text","value":">"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"returns-2","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Returns"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Promise"},{"type":"text","value":"<"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Awaited"},{"type":"text","value":"<"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReturnType"},{"type":"text","value":"<"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFn"},{"type":"text","value":">> | "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":">"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A promise that resolves with the function's return value, or undefined if an error occurred and was handled by onError"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"throws","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Throws"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The error from the rate-limited function if no onError handler is configured"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"example","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const rateLimiter = new AsyncRateLimiter(fn, { limit: 5, window: 1000 });\n\n// First 5 calls will return a promise that resolves with the result\nconst result = await rateLimiter.maybeExecute('arg1', 'arg2');\n\n// Additional calls within the window will return undefined\nconst result2 = await rateLimiter.maybeExecute('arg1', 'arg2'); // undefined","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"example-2","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Rate limit to 5 calls per minute with a sliding window\nconst rateLimited = asyncRateLimit(makeApiCall, {\n  limit: 5,\n  window: 60000,\n  windowType: 'sliding',\n  onError: (error) => {\n    console.error('API call failed:', error);\n  },\n  onReject: (rateLimiter) => {\n    console.log(`Rate limit exceeded. Try again in ${rateLimiter.getMsUntilNextWindow()}ms`);\n  }\n});\n\n// First 5 calls will execute immediately\n// Additional calls will be rejected until the minute window resets\n// Returns the API response directly\nconst result = await rateLimited();\n\n// For more even execution, consider using throttle instead:\nconst throttled = throttle(makeApiCall, { wait: 12000 }); // One call every 12 seconds","lang":"ts"}],"headings":[{"id":"type-parameters","text":"Type Parameters","level":2},{"id":"tfn","text":"TFn","level":3},{"id":"parameters","text":"Parameters","level":2},{"id":"fn","text":"fn","level":3},{"id":"initialoptions","text":"initialOptions","level":3},{"id":"returns","text":"Returns","level":2},{"id":"parameters-2","text":"Parameters","level":3},{"id":"args","text":"args","level":4},{"id":"returns-2","text":"Returns","level":3},{"id":"throws","text":"Throws","level":3},{"id":"example","text":"Example","level":3},{"id":"example-2","text":"Example","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n```ts\nfunction asyncRateLimit<TFn>(fn, initialOptions): (...args) => Promise<Awaited<ReturnType<TFn>> | undefined>;\n```\n\nDefined in: [async-rate-limiter.ts:642](https://github.com/TanStack/pacer/blob/main/packages/pacer/src/async-rate-limiter.ts#L642)\n\nCreates an async rate-limited function that will execute the provided function up to a maximum number of times within a time window.\n\nAsync vs Sync Versions:\nThe async version provides advanced features over the sync rate limit function:\n- Returns promises that can be awaited for rate-limited function results\n- Built-in retry support via AsyncRetryer integration\n- Abort support to cancel in-flight executions\n- Comprehensive error handling with onError callbacks and throwOnError control\n- Detailed execution tracking (success/error/settle counts, rejection counts)\n- More sophisticated window management with automatic cleanup\n\nThe sync rate limit function is lighter weight and simpler when you don't need async features,\nreturn values, or execution control.\n\nWhat is Rate Limiting?\nRate limiting allows a function to execute up to a limit within a time window,\nthen blocks all subsequent calls until the window passes. This can lead to \"bursty\" behavior where\nall executions happen immediately, followed by a complete block.\n\nWindow Types:\n- 'fixed': A strict window that resets after the window period. All executions within the window count\n  towards the limit, and the window resets completely after the period.\n- 'sliding': A rolling window that allows executions as old ones expire. This provides a more\n  consistent rate of execution over time.\n\nConfiguration Options:\n- `limit`: Maximum number of executions allowed within the window (required)\n- `window`: Time window in milliseconds (required)\n- `windowType`: 'fixed' or 'sliding' (default: 'fixed')\n- `enabled`: Whether the rate limiter is enabled (default: true)\n- `asyncRetryerOptions`: Configure retry behavior for executions\n\nWhen to Use Rate Limiting:\nRate limiting is best used for hard API limits or resource constraints. For UI updates or\nsmoothing out frequent events, throttling or debouncing usually provide better user experience.\n- A rate limiter will allow all executions until the limit is reached, then block all subsequent calls until the window resets\n- A throttler ensures even spacing between executions, which can be better for consistent performance\n- A debouncer collapses multiple calls into one, which is better for handling bursts of events\n\nError Handling:\n- If an `onError` handler is provided, it will be called with the error and rate limiter instance\n- If `throwOnError` is true (default when no onError handler is provided), the error will be thrown\n- If `throwOnError` is false (default when onError handler is provided), the error will be swallowed\n- Both onError and throwOnError can be used together - the handler will be called before any error is thrown\n- The error state can be checked using the underlying AsyncRateLimiter instance\n- Rate limit rejections (when limit is exceeded) are handled separately from execution errors via the `onReject` handler\n\nState Management:\n- Uses TanStack Store for reactive state management\n- Use `initialState` to provide initial state values when creating the rate limiter\n- `initialState` can be a partial state object\n- Use `onSuccess` callback to react to successful function execution and implement custom logic\n- Use `onError` callback to react to function execution errors and implement custom error handling\n- Use `onSettled` callback to react to function execution completion (success or error) and implement custom logic\n- Use `onReject` callback to react to executions being rejected when rate limit is exceeded\n- The state includes execution times, success/error counts, and current execution status\n- State can be accessed via the underlying AsyncRateLimiter instance's `store.state` property\n- When using framework adapters (React/Solid), state is accessed from the hook's state property\n\n## Type Parameters\n\n### TFn\n\n`TFn` *extends* [`AnyAsyncFunction`](../type-aliases/AnyAsyncFunction.md)\n\n## Parameters\n\n### fn\n\n`TFn`\n\n### initialOptions\n\n[`AsyncRateLimiterOptions`](../interfaces/AsyncRateLimiterOptions.md)\\<`TFn`\\>\n\n## Returns\n\n```ts\n(...args): Promise<Awaited<ReturnType<TFn>> | undefined>;\n```\n\nAttempts to execute the rate-limited function if within the configured limits.\nWill reject execution if the number of calls in the current window exceeds the limit.\n\nError Handling:\n- If the rate-limited function throws and no `onError` handler is configured,\n  the error will be thrown from this method.\n- If an `onError` handler is configured, errors will be caught and passed to the handler,\n  and this method will return undefined.\n- The error state can be checked using `getErrorCount()` and `getIsExecuting()`.\n\n### Parameters\n\n#### args\n\n...`Parameters`\\<`TFn`\\>\n\n### Returns\n\n`Promise`\\<`Awaited`\\<`ReturnType`\\<`TFn`\\>\\> \\| `undefined`\\>\n\nA promise that resolves with the function's return value, or undefined if an error occurred and was handled by onError\n\n### Throws\n\nThe error from the rate-limited function if no onError handler is configured\n\n### Example\n\n```ts\nconst rateLimiter = new AsyncRateLimiter(fn, { limit: 5, window: 1000 });\n\n// First 5 calls will return a promise that resolves with the result\nconst result = await rateLimiter.maybeExecute('arg1', 'arg2');\n\n// Additional calls within the window will return undefined\nconst result2 = await rateLimiter.maybeExecute('arg1', 'arg2'); // undefined\n```\n\n## Example\n\n```ts\n// Rate limit to 5 calls per minute with a sliding window\nconst rateLimited = asyncRateLimit(makeApiCall, {\n  limit: 5,\n  window: 60000,\n  windowType: 'sliding',\n  onError: (error) => {\n    console.error('API call failed:', error);\n  },\n  onReject: (rateLimiter) => {\n    console.log(`Rate limit exceeded. Try again in ${rateLimiter.getMsUntilNextWindow()}ms`);\n  }\n});\n\n// First 5 calls will execute immediately\n// Additional calls will be rejected until the minute window resets\n// Returns the API response directly\nconst result = await rateLimited();\n\n// For more even execution, consider using throttle instead:\nconst throttled = throttle(makeApiCall, { wait: 12000 }); // One call every 12 seconds\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: asyncRateLimit\ntitle: asyncRateLimit\n---\n\n```ts\nfunction asyncRateLimit<TFn>(fn, initialOptions): (...args) => Promise<Awaited<ReturnType<TFn>> | undefined>;\n```\n\nDefined in: [async-rate-limiter.ts:642](https://github.com/TanStack/pacer/blob/main/packages/pacer/src/async-rate-limiter.ts#L642)\n\nCreates an async rate-limited function that will execute the provided function up to a maximum number of times within a time window.\n\nAsync vs Sync Versions:\nThe async version provides advanced features over the sync rate limit function:\n- Returns promises that can be awaited for rate-limited function results\n- Built-in retry support via AsyncRetryer integration\n- Abort support to cancel in-flight executions\n- Comprehensive error handling with onError callbacks and throwOnError control\n- Detailed execution tracking (success/error/settle counts, rejection counts)\n- More sophisticated window management with automatic cleanup\n\nThe sync rate limit function is lighter weight and simpler when you don't need async features,\nreturn values, or execution control.\n\nWhat is Rate Limiting?\nRate limiting allows a function to execute up to a limit within a time window,\nthen blocks all subsequent calls until the window passes. This can lead to \"bursty\" behavior where\nall executions happen immediately, followed by a complete block.\n\nWindow Types:\n- 'fixed': A strict window that resets after the window period. All executions within the window count\n  towards the limit, and the window resets completely after the period.\n- 'sliding': A rolling window that allows executions as old ones expire. This provides a more\n  consistent rate of execution over time.\n\nConfiguration Options:\n- `limit`: Maximum number of executions allowed within the window (required)\n- `window`: Time window in milliseconds (required)\n- `windowType`: 'fixed' or 'sliding' (default: 'fixed')\n- `enabled`: Whether the rate limiter is enabled (default: true)\n- `asyncRetryerOptions`: Configure retry behavior for executions\n\nWhen to Use Rate Limiting:\nRate limiting is best used for hard API limits or resource constraints. For UI updates or\nsmoothing out frequent events, throttling or debouncing usually provide better user experience.\n- A rate limiter will allow all executions until the limit is reached, then block all subsequent calls until the window resets\n- A throttler ensures even spacing between executions, which can be better for consistent performance\n- A debouncer collapses multiple calls into one, which is better for handling bursts of events\n\nError Handling:\n- If an `onError` handler is provided, it will be called with the error and rate limiter instance\n- If `throwOnError` is true (default when no onError handler is provided), the error will be thrown\n- If `throwOnError` is false (default when onError handler is provided), the error will be swallowed\n- Both onError and throwOnError can be used together - the handler will be called before any error is thrown\n- The error state can be checked using the underlying AsyncRateLimiter instance\n- Rate limit rejections (when limit is exceeded) are handled separately from execution errors via the `onReject` handler\n\nState Management:\n- Uses TanStack Store for reactive state management\n- Use `initialState` to provide initial state values when creating the rate limiter\n- `initialState` can be a partial state object\n- Use `onSuccess` callback to react to successful function execution and implement custom logic\n- Use `onError` callback to react to function execution errors and implement custom error handling\n- Use `onSettled` callback to react to function execution completion (success or error) and implement custom logic\n- Use `onReject` callback to react to executions being rejected when rate limit is exceeded\n- The state includes execution times, success/error counts, and current execution status\n- State can be accessed via the underlying AsyncRateLimiter instance's `store.state` property\n- When using framework adapters (React/Solid), state is accessed from the hook's state property\n\n## Type Parameters\n\n### TFn\n\n`TFn` *extends* [`AnyAsyncFunction`](../type-aliases/AnyAsyncFunction.md)\n\n## Parameters\n\n### fn\n\n`TFn`\n\n### initialOptions\n\n[`AsyncRateLimiterOptions`](../interfaces/AsyncRateLimiterOptions.md)\\<`TFn`\\>\n\n## Returns\n\n```ts\n(...args): Promise<Awaited<ReturnType<TFn>> | undefined>;\n```\n\nAttempts to execute the rate-limited function if within the configured limits.\nWill reject execution if the number of calls in the current window exceeds the limit.\n\nError Handling:\n- If the rate-limited function throws and no `onError` handler is configured,\n  the error will be thrown from this method.\n- If an `onError` handler is configured, errors will be caught and passed to the handler,\n  and this method will return undefined.\n- The error state can be checked using `getErrorCount()` and `getIsExecuting()`.\n\n### Parameters\n\n#### args\n\n...`Parameters`\\<`TFn`\\>\n\n### Returns\n\n`Promise`\\<`Awaited`\\<`ReturnType`\\<`TFn`\\>\\> \\| `undefined`\\>\n\nA promise that resolves with the function's return value, or undefined if an error occurred and was handled by onError\n\n### Throws\n\nThe error from the rate-limited function if no onError handler is configured\n\n### Example\n\n```ts\nconst rateLimiter = new AsyncRateLimiter(fn, { limit: 5, window: 1000 });\n\n// First 5 calls will return a promise that resolves with the result\nconst result = await rateLimiter.maybeExecute('arg1', 'arg2');\n\n// Additional calls within the window will return undefined\nconst result2 = await rateLimiter.maybeExecute('arg1', 'arg2'); // undefined\n```\n\n## Example\n\n```ts\n// Rate limit to 5 calls per minute with a sliding window\nconst rateLimited = asyncRateLimit(makeApiCall, {\n  limit: 5,\n  window: 60000,\n  windowType: 'sliding',\n  onError: (error) => {\n    console.error('API call failed:', error);\n  },\n  onReject: (rateLimiter) => {\n    console.log(`Rate limit exceeded. Try again in ${rateLimiter.getMsUntilNextWindow()}ms`);\n  }\n});\n\n// First 5 calls will execute immediately\n// Additional calls will be rejected until the minute window resets\n// Returns the API response directly\nconst result = await rateLimited();\n\n// For more even execution, consider using throttle instead:\nconst throttled = throttle(makeApiCall, { wait: 12000 }); // One call every 12 seconds\n```\n"}
